  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-06-26T20:09:14.537Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Animators are probrably some of the most powerfull tools we have on avatars right now (cause let's be honest, apart from that and shaders there isn't too much to play with), and some people achieved some really cool effects with those, like entire inventories, or permanent transformations that stay after the animation is complete. \n\nBut the way most of these effects are done is by using a kinda hacked in way to enable and disabling animators, and using that to control the animation state flow. \n\nThis comes with quite a bit of disadvantages, for example the need to disable the animator to keep its state, or the fact that enabling-disabling the animator is the only real input we can give to it, so no branching into different states and no real way to alter the flow of the states, apart from stopping and restarting it.\n\nSo the idea would be to have an avatar component (be it a separate component or integrated in the avatar descriptor) that enables us to interact a bit more with animators, and could be done in 2 ways:\n\n1) Having the component set to do operations with animator parameters when an emote or gesture is played (similar to how a vrc_trigger interacts with animators in maps).\n\n2) Having the component directly passing the animator parameters used into the main humanoid animator (like the emote, gesture, movementx, movementz parametes) into selected animators, ending up having the animators contain the same parameters of the main humanoid animator and being updated whenever we do an action.\n\nI personally prefer the second option cause would give the greatest benefit in terms of control. but eighter option would give way more control than what we have now.",
